========================================================================
RESIDUALS (honest, out of assigned red-team)
========================================================================

1. **2.9 unify OPEN.** Stmt `println` does not call `c_secret_emit_guard`.
   Real land is `c_emit_println` after args are lowered (or drop the
   ident special-case so stmt println hits `c_emit_lower`). Check-path
   cannot call the guard without C arg text.
2. **DE2.9 table is false.** Do not treat SPRINT “What got fixed” as a
   close. Hygiene already said this.
3. **Stmt vs eprintln split.** `eprintln(SECRET)` hits the guard via
   lower. Stmt `println` does not. That is the ticket.
4. **Check walker LPAREN optional.** Accidental forward scan + true
   alias resolve both refuse P04. Unify should keep **true** alias
   (`resolve` then scan the call’s args), not the file-forward scan.
5. **`c_emit_println` arg refuse is `__sec__` only** (no alias resolve
   on the printed name). emit-c is saved by check-first. If check is
   ever skipped, alias-of-data at stmt println is weaker than the guard.
6. **s61 official smoke exit 1** on this host (check type-first). emit-c
   still refuse. WT reorder not in ELF. Not 2.9.
7. **P04c emit-c abort** (`malloc(): unaligned tcache chunk`) on
   `let f = println` without a call. Check OK. Host emit residual.
   Not a SECRET hole.
8. **`${}` interp** — 5.8. String text is correctly not IDENT.
9. **User-sink wrappers** — 2.10. Not this OWN.
10. Full IFC / `#[Secret]` / interproc beyond `__fr_secret__` — named
    in `SECRET_TAINT.oot`. Do not greenwash.

